<ShortAnswer>
  open the OneTab page, click Export / Import URLs at the top right, copy all the text and save it
  as a .txt file. That file is your backup, and TabCabin imports it as it is: Settings → Data →
  Import from a file.
</ShortAnswer>

<h2>Why export at all</h2>
<p>
  OneTab keeps its lists inside the browser, on this computer only. It has no account and no copy
  anywhere else. When the extension is removed, the browser profile is reset or the computer is
  replaced, the lists go with it. An export is the only backup there is.
</p>

<h2>1. Export the lists</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Right-click the OneTab icon and choose Display OneTab.</li>
  <li>At the top right of the page, click Export / Import URLs.</li>
  <li>
    Click in the box with the addresses, select everything with <kbd>Ctrl+A</kbd> and copy it with
    <kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd>.
  </li>
  <li>Paste it into a text editor and save it as a file, for example onetab.txt.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Each line is one tab: the address, a vertical bar and the title. A blank line starts the next
  group. Stars and locks are not part of the file, so note which groups mattered before you move.
</p>

<h2>2. Keep the file somewhere safe</h2>
<p>
  Put it in a cloud drive or send it to yourself. OneTab adds new lists over time, so export again
  every few weeks, or before you reinstall the browser.
</p>

<h2>3. Bring the lists into TabCabin</h2>
<p>
  A text file is a backup you can restore, but not a place to work. TabCabin is a free, open-source
  extension that turns the new tab page into boards, and it reads the OneTab file directly.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Get TabCabin for Chrome, Edge or Brave and open a new tab.</li>
  <li>Open Settings → Data → Import from a file and choose the .txt file.</li>
  <li>
    The tabs arrive in a new workspace, “Imported from OneTab”, with each OneTab group as a group of
    its own.
  </li>
  <li>
    Rename the groups, drag them where they belong and add a note to whatever you want to remember.
  </li>
</ol>
